course: dictionary
lesson: 13624
title: A formal word: indisposition
level: primary-5
stars: 3
explain: Indisposition is a formal word. It means a slight illness, like a cold or an upset stomach, that stops you from doing things as usual. «Mum had a slight indisposition, so she stayed in bed with a cold.» Mum was poorly, not badly hurt, so indisposition means a mild illness.
explain: The word can also mean unwillingness, which is not wanting to do something. «He felt an indisposition to tidy his room, so he sat on his bed and read instead.» Nobody was ill there. The clue is that he did not want to do the job, so the word means unwillingness this time.
explain: You will mostly meet indisposition in announcements and formal letters. Instead of saying «the singer is ill», a letter might say «due to the singer's indisposition, the show is cancelled». When you speak every day, you could say «feeling poorly» or «under the weather» instead.
fact: Indisposition is a noun, so you can say «an indisposition» or «indispositions».
fact: «Indisposition» means a slight illness that stops you doing things, or unwillingness to do something.
fact: Useful phrases: «a slight indisposition» and «due to indisposition».
fact: Everyday words with a similar meaning to the illness sense are «feeling poorly» and «under the weather».
fact: Formal words like indisposition are often used in announcements, letters and speeches, to sound polite and serious.
exercise: «Mum had a slight indisposition, so she stayed in bed.» What does indisposition mean here? | A new coat | A big party | *A mild illness | A long holiday
exercise: Which everyday phrase means about the same as «a slight indisposition»? | *Feeling a bit poorly | Very excited about the trip | Running very fast | Getting a new bike
exercise: «He felt an indisposition to tidy his room.» What does this suggest? | *He did not want to tidy it | He had already tidied it | He had a bad cold | He was very keen to tidy it
test: «Due to the coach's indisposition, the match is postponed.» What is the most likely meaning? | The match is moved to the sea | A holiday for the coach | *The coach is slightly ill | The coach has won a prize
test: Dad says, «I have an indisposition towards cleaning the car today.» What does he mean? | *He does not want to clean it | He has cleaned it already | He is ill in bed | He loves washing cars
exam: Which sentence uses indisposition correctly? | We went to the indisposition after school | The indisposition was a green bicycle | *Due to my indisposition, I will miss the trip | I had a big indisposition of cake
